 "column_legend": {
  "first_occurrence_notes": "distinct root notes (earliest statements) that later notes of this category are aligned with; k = printed",
  "later_occurrence_notes": "notes in later occurrences of this category; k = printed",
  "p_later_printed_given_first_printed": "aligned note pairs; n = pairs whose root note is printed, k = of those, later note printed",
  "p_later_printed_given_first_unprinted": "n = pairs whose root note is unprinted, k = later printed",
  "same_digit_given_both_printed": "share of pairs with both printed whose digits are equal",
  "baseline_no_repeat": "notes in no repeated segment (neither a later note nor a root of one)",
  "categories": "kinds (exact, transposed_*, mixed_shift) are relative to the root events; adjacent/distant relative to the direct first occurrence; len_* by occurrence length in events"
 },
 "caveats": [
  "The Mutopia converter keeps volta/segno repeats in written order (printed once) and expands \\repeat unfold; a passage written with repeat signs is therefore not a repeat here, and an unfolded block carries the editor's digits on every copy by construction (LilyPond engraves them on every copy too, so that is what the reader sees).",
  "Matching is chromatic (constant semitone shift); diatonic sequences whose interval qualities change are not detected.",
  "Four-event matches include generic figuration (Alberti cells, scale fragments, repeated chords); the len_ge_8 / len_ge_16 / exact_len_ge_8 rows are closer to 'repeated passage' in the musical sense. Periodic figuration produces doubling segments (4, 8, 16, ... events), so long segments are not always phrases.",
  "Greedy left-to-right search: a short match at j can pre-empt a longer one starting at j+1.",
  "Library pieces are excerpts of pieces also present in the Mutopia corpus."
 ]
}